Mathematical Foundations for Reasoning as Constrained Gradient Flow
A Theoretical Framework

ouadi Maakoul 

1. Introduction

This work establishes a mathematical framework in which reasoning is formalised as a constrained gradient flow on a cognitive state manifold. The construction synthesises three recently validated pillars:

· Geometric cognition (Ale, 2025) – cognitive states are points on a Riemannian manifold whose metric encodes representational constraints; thought unfolds as the gradient flow of a scalar potential.
· Energy-based reasoning (Gladstone et al., 2025) – transformer architectures learn an energy landscape and refine predictions through gradient‑based optimisation, instantiating a form of “System 2” deliberation.
· Gödel‑Boolean homomorphism (Daniele & van Krieken, 2026) – gradient descent on a Gödel‑logic relaxation of a satisfiability problem corresponds exactly to a discrete local search, bridging continuous optimisation and logical deduction.

To these we add rigorous tools from stability theory (Mohammadi et al., 2024; Chen et al., 2026), spectral graph analysis (Kelner et al., 2008; Tishkov, 2026), discretisation of gradient flows (Zhang et al., 2025; Jüngel et al., 2019), and metastability theory for random energy landscapes (Bovier et al., 2004; Dandi et al., 2025).

The principal contribution is a unified energy functional whose minimisers correspond to coherent, logically admissible reasoning states, and whose gradient‑flow dynamics combine semantic coherence, structural parsimony, logical consistency, uncertainty reduction, and memory retention. The framework is supported by rigorous proofs of existence, stability, discretisation convergence, and the isomorphism between continuous optimisation and discrete logical search.

