3. The Cognitive State Manifold

Following Ale (2025), we model the cognitive state as a point on a Riemannian manifold. To ground the subsequent existence and compactness proofs we explicitly construct \mathcal{M} as a compact product of factor spaces, each encoding a distinct facet of a reasoning state:

\mathcal{M} \;=\; \mathbb{S}^{d_{\text{sem}}-1} \;\times\; [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} \;\times\; \Delta^{k_{\text{ent}}-1},

where:

· \mathbb{S}^{d_{\text{sem}}-1} is the unit sphere in \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{sem}}}, hosting semantic embeddings normalised to unit length (cf. Gladstone et al., 2025).
· [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} is the hypercube of logical truth values, one dimension per propositional variable; it carries the Gödel relaxations of Boolean constraints.
· \Delta^{k_{\text{ent}}-1} is the probability simplex of dimension k_{\text{ent}}-1, representing the predictive distribution over the next reasoning step.

All three factors are compact and convex (or closed) subsets of Euclidean spaces; hence \mathcal{M} is a compact C^\infty manifold with corners. We equip \mathcal{M} with a product Riemannian metric

g = g_{\text{sem}} \oplus g_{\text{log}} \oplus g_{\text{ent}},

where g_{\text{sem}} is the round metric on the sphere, g_{\text{log}} the Euclidean metric restricted to the hypercube, and g_{\text{ent}} the Fisher information metric on the simplex. The product structure ensures that \mathcal{M} is complete and compact, thus every continuous function attains its minimum (see Theorem 4.1).

Remark. The use of a fixed product metric is a minimal setting; Ale (2025) allows the metric to be learned from data. The theory developed here remains valid for any smoothly varying metric on this compact base space.

