5. Existence and Stability of Minimisers

Theorem 5.1 (Existence and compactness of global minimisers).
The functional E_{\text{total}} attains its global minimum on \mathcal{M}, and the set of minimisers \mathcal{M}^* = \{s \in \mathcal{M} : E_{\text{total}}(s) = \min E_{\text{total}}\} is compact.

Proof. \mathcal{M} is compact by construction (product of compact sets). E_{\text{total}} is continuous. By the Extreme Value Theorem, the minimum is attained. The set \mathcal{M}^* = E_{\text{total}}^{-1}(\{\min E_{\text{total}}\}) is the pre‑image of a closed set under a continuous map, hence closed; a closed subset of a compact space is compact. ∎

Remark. If one wishes to work with a non‑compact state space (e.g., all of \mathbb{R}^{d_{\text{sem}}}), compactness can be replaced by coercivity: if E_{\text{total}}(s) \to \infty as \operatorname{dist}(s,s_0) \to \infty, then sublevel sets are compact and minimisers still exist. In that case the memory term E_{\text{mem}} or an added confining potential provides coercivity. For simplicity we retain the compact manifold.

Theorem 5.2 (Exponential stability of strict local minima).
Assume E \in C^2(\mathcal{M}) and let s^* be a strict local minimum with \nabla E(s^*) = 0 and \operatorname{Hess}_g E(s^*) \succeq \lambda I for some \lambda > 0. Then s^* is exponentially stable for the gradient flow \dot{s} = -\nabla E(s).

Proof. Define the Lyapunov function V(s) = E(s) - E(s^*). In a neighbourhood U of s^*, V(s) > 0 for s \neq s^*. Along trajectories,

\dot{V}(s(t)) = \langle \nabla E(s), \dot{s} \rangle_g = -\|\nabla E(s)\|_g^2 \le 0,

so V is a Lyapunov function, giving Lyapunov stability.

Working in normal coordinates centred at s^*, write v = \exp_{s^*}^{-1}(s). Taylor expansion yields

E(s) = E(s^*) + \frac12 g(\operatorname{Hess}_g E(s^*) v, v) + o(\|v\|_g^2).

From the lower bound on the Hessian, for sufficiently small \|v\|_g,

V(s) = E(s)-E(s^*) \ge \frac{\lambda}{4} \|v\|_g^2,
\qquad
\|\nabla E(s)\|_g^2 \ge \frac{\lambda^2}{2} \|v\|_g^2 \ge 2\lambda V(s).

Hence \dot{V} = -\|\nabla E\|_g^2 \le -2\lambda V locally. Grönwall’s inequality gives V(s(t)) \le V(s_0) e^{-2\lambda t}, and because \operatorname{dist}_g(s,s^*)^2 \asymp V(s) locally, we obtain

\operatorname{dist}_g(s(t), s^*) \le C e^{-\alpha t} \operatorname{dist}_g(s_0, s^*)

for some C,\alpha>0. Thus s^* is exponentially stable. ∎

Remark 5.3 (Extension to non‑smooth energies).
When E is only piecewise C^2 due to the Gödel \max, the above argument can be rescued if E satisfies the Kurdyka–Łojasiewicz inequality at s^* with exponent \theta = 1/2. This follows from the fact that the smoothing E^\epsilon satisfies uniform Hessian bounds independent of \epsilon, and the set of non‑differentiable points has measure zero (Daniele & van Krieken, 2026; Attouch et al., 2013).

