6. The Gödel–Boolean Homomorphism and Differentiable Logical Search

Theorem 6.1 (Gödel–Boolean homomorphism).
Let \mathcal{C} be a set of Boolean constraints in CNF. The Gödel relaxation \tilde{\mathcal{C}} : [0,1]^{n_{\text{log}}} \to [0,1]^m defined above satisfies:

1. \tilde{\mathcal{C}}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbf{1} iff all clauses are classically satisfied by the Boolean projection \mathbf{x} \in \{0,1\}^{n_{\text{log}}}.
2. At points where a clause is not satisfied, the gradient \nabla \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) is non‑zero only on the single literal that attains the maximum; consequently \nabla E_{\text{symb}} is sparse.
3. Gradient descent on E_{\text{symb}} (or its smoothing) implements a discrete local search for a satisfying assignment: each step identifies a currently unsatisfied clause and modifies exactly one variable within it.

Proof. (Adapted from Daniele & van Krieken, 2026). For a clause C_k = \bigvee_{i\in P_k} x_i \lor \bigvee_{j\in N_k} \neg x_j, the Gödel value is \tilde{C}_k(\mathbf{x}) = \max(\max_{i\in P_k} x_i, \max_{j\in N_k} (1-x_j)). When \mathbf{x} \in \{0,1\}^n, this value is 1 exactly when at least one positive literal is true or one negative literal is false, which is precisely the classical satisfiability condition. Because the \max function is piecewise linear, its subgradient is concentrated on the argmax literal. For a clause that is not satisfied (value <1), the argmax is unique almost everywhere; therefore \partial \tilde{C}_k / \partial x_i is non‑zero for at most one variable per clause. A gradient step x_i \leftarrow x_i - \eta \,\partial E_{\text{symb}}/\partial x_i therefore moves a single variable, mimicking the flip of a WalkSAT solver. The smoothing \tilde{C}_k^\epsilon concentrates its gradient sharply around the maximal literal, preserving this one‑variable update property as \epsilon \to 0. ∎

Corollary 6.2 (Admissible submanifold).
The set \mathcal{A} = \{s \in \mathcal{M} : E_{\text{symb}}(s)=0\} is a closed subset of \mathcal{M} representing all logically admissible states. Its smoothed approximation \mathcal{A}_\epsilon = \{s : E_{\text{symb}}^\epsilon(s)=0\} is a C^\infty submanifold, and \mathcal{A}_\epsilon \to \mathcal{A} in Hausdorff distance as \epsilon \to 0.

Thus the gradient flow of E_{\text{total}} naturally incorporates logical constraints as a differentiable penalty that drives the state towards \mathcal{A}, and the dynamics on \mathcal{A} corresponds to a continuous relaxation of a discrete local search.

