8. Spectral Regularisation via the Graph Laplacian

The topological energy acts as a regulariser that encourages smoothness with respect to a knowledge graph. The following spectral bounds show how the geometry of the graph controls the strength of this regularisation.

Theorem 8.1 (Spectral bounds for E_{\text{topo}}).
For a connected weighted knowledge graph \mathcal{G} with Laplacian L,

\lambda_2 \|\iota_{\mathcal{G}}(s) - \bar{\iota}\|^2 \le E_{\text{topo}}(s) \le \lambda_{\max} \|\iota_{\mathcal{G}}(s)\|^2,

where \lambda_2 is the algebraic connectivity and \lambda_{\max} the spectral radius. Hence high‑frequency graph modes are strongly penalised, and the flow tends to align activations with the graph’s low‑frequency eigenvectors.

Proof. By the Courant–Fischer theorem, \lambda_2 = \min_{x \perp \mathbf{1}} x^\top L x / \|x\|^2 and \lambda_{\max} = \max_{x} x^\top L x / \|x\|^2. Substituting \iota_{\mathcal{G}}(s) and noting that the constant vector \mathbf{1} is the eigenvector for \lambda_1=0 gives the bounds. ∎

The lower bound shows that if the algebraic connectivity \lambda_2 is large, the topological energy forces a consensus state, providing exponential mixing of information across the graph (Cheeger inequality). This connects reasoning on the graph to the geometric mixing properties of the manifold.

